WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.577 --> 00:00:04.410
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hi.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.480
I'm Dustin Kirkland,
product manager at Google,

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.610
and I'm here with my
colleague, JD Velasquez,

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.842
product manager at
Google for Stackdriver.

00:00:10.842 --> 00:00:13.050
He's going to tell us about
the exciting announcement

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.034
from earlier today.

00:00:14.034 --> 00:00:15.450
JD VELASQUEZ: So
we just announced

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.699
the beta release of Stackdriver
Kubernetes Monitoring,

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:21.240
monitoring which provides rich
and comprehensive observability

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.640
of your Kubernetes environment,
simplifying operations

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.070
for both developers
and operators.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:27.570
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
That's interesting.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.387
So Stackdriver is a GKE-specific
monitoring solution?

00:00:31.387 --> 00:00:32.220
JD VELASQUEZ: Oh no.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.110
This is actually one
of the great things

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.310
about this announcement
and solution.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.666
It is pre-integrated with
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:38.666 --> 00:00:42.450
but it can be configured with
any Kubernetes deployments,

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.650
no matter where they are.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.190
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Any Kubernetes
deployment, that's interesting.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.210
JD VELASQUEZ: On other
cloud providers, or on prem.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:49.751
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Now how does
this tie back to my Prometheus,

00:00:49.751 --> 00:00:50.820
for instance?

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.028
JD VELASQUEZ: Great question.

00:00:52.028 --> 00:00:54.600
So the Stackdrive Kubernetes
Monitoring solution

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.726
integrates and works very
well with open source.

00:00:56.726 --> 00:00:58.350
We have an integration
with Prometheus.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.662
So if you have your
application configured,

00:01:01.662 --> 00:01:03.120
an instrument to
be Prometheus, you

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.550
can bring that into Stackdriver
without modification.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:07.560
So now you have a
single place where

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.460
you can have a unified solution
with metrics, logs, events,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.980
and metadata, by your Kubernetes
environment, including

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.520
application and metrics,
as well as custom metrics,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.810
into a single place, to give
you that rich observability.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.010
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And how
difficult is it to turn on?

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.260
If I want to start
monitoring, I want Stackdriver

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.860
monitoring my existing cluster?

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.360
What does that
experience look like?

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.860
JD VELASQUEZ: Right,
it's very easy.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:32.070
It's-- you get that
observability right from

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:32.640
the box.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.400
As soon as you have a--

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.850
your Kubernetes
deployment, you're

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.744
just going to enable
monitoring, Stackdriver, done.

00:01:38.744 --> 00:01:39.660
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Cool.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.670
And what about the chattiness?

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.220
How chatty is Stackdriver?

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.070
There's a lot of
data to be logged.

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:46.020
How does that work?

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.520
JD VELASQUEZ: There's
a lot of data,

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.890
but that's the
important aspect of it.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.290
So this unified
solution will get

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.550
logs out of your Kubernetes
environment, metrics as well,

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:56.530
and events.

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:58.630
But we give them in
the right context.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.370
So, for instance, if
you're a developer,

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.828
you want to get that data to
understand the normal behavior

00:02:02.828 --> 00:02:04.020
of your application, right?

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.410
And to do analysis and
diagnosis when things go wrong.

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.630
If you're an operator,
you can use that same data

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.010
in a different view
in the right context.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.629
So you monitor multiple
clusters in the single place,

00:02:15.629 --> 00:02:17.670
and no matter if you have
an infrastructure view,

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.260
you want to look at your
clusters and your nodes

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.218
specifically, or you
really want to take a look

00:02:21.218 --> 00:02:22.710
at your workloads
and appointments,

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.520
or even at your
coordinated services.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:26.800
So all of this is integrated
in the right place.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:27.716
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Cool.

00:02:27.716 --> 00:02:30.134
And can I catch events
and actually take actions

00:02:30.134 --> 00:02:31.050
based on those events?

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:34.104
Maybe scale up, scale
down, spin another cluster?

00:02:34.104 --> 00:02:36.270
JD VELASQUEZ: Right, so at
the moment with the beta,

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.340
we're surfacing events
based on incidents.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.015
And incidents are violations
of alerting policies

00:02:41.015 --> 00:02:41.979
that you may have.

00:02:41.979 --> 00:02:44.520
And so, but they're correlated,
so you have an event timeline

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.430
that you can show if
there was an incident,

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.846
then you can correlate it with
the state of your company's

00:02:48.846 --> 00:02:50.792
environment to more
easily get to the root

00:02:50.792 --> 00:02:52.000
cause of any of these issues.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.750
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
those policies, can I

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:54.900
define my own policies?

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:55.733
JD VELASQUEZ: Right.

00:02:55.733 --> 00:02:58.200
That's a great thing with
Stackdriver and the Kubernetes

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.330
Monitoring Solution
that we just announced,

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:01.880
is that it works
great standalone,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.380
but it works even
better when you're

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.120
integrating with the rest
of the Stackdriver products.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.730
So, for instance, you have
powerful and comprehensive

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.680
learning system, with
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.810
where you can then pursue
a hypothesis if you're

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.000
trying to root cause
an issue and deep dive

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.490
into logs in
Stackdriver Logging.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.050
Or, even go beyond,
and try to see

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:22.050
latency analysis with
Stackdriver Trace,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.692
or consumption of your resources
with Stackdriver Profiler.

00:03:24.692 --> 00:03:26.400
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And
what about BigQuery?

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.010
Can I tie all this back
to BigQuery, perhaps?

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.140
JD VELASQUEZ: Yes, so in
the Stackdriver Logging

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:35.490
has already exports that you can
make to BigQuery Google Cloud

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:36.830
Storage and Pub/Sub.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.600
And so usually, many of our
customers take those logs,

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.302
export them to BigQuery, and do
a deeper analysis on BigQuery.

00:03:42.302 --> 00:03:43.510
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Interesting.

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:44.354
Do you have a demo?

00:03:44.354 --> 00:03:46.770
Maybe you could show us a
little bit about how this works?

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:47.150
JD VELASQUEZ: I do.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:48.024
Let me show you that.

00:03:48.024 --> 00:03:50.044
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: All right.

00:03:50.044 --> 00:03:52.210
JD VELASQUEZ: So this is
the Stackdriver Community's

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:53.440
monitoring console.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.910
And what you have here is
an integration of events

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.610
and various signals
metrics, logs,

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.500
and so forth in a single place.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.720
So the event timeline
shows incidents

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.660
that may have happened,
and if you're an operator,

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:09.670
you would look at perhaps
the infrastructure.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.460
So you would go from your
cluster to your node,

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:13.360
and so forth.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:15.130
If you were a
developer, you may be

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.059
more interested in your
workloads and deployments.

00:04:18.059 --> 00:04:19.600
And so, you can go
from your clusters

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.029
to name spaces, and
pods, and containers.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.400
Or, perhaps you go to
your services from cluster

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.160
to name spaces,
pods, and containers.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.690
Once you're interested
in something,

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.890
you can inspect the
company's object further.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:33.500
So I'm looking here at
a particular container.

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:36.880
and I'll see key metrics and
all the logs that are associated

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.740
with that container.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.430
Stackdriver
Communities Monitoring

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.950
works great standalone,
but it works better

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:45.210
with all the other
Stackdriver products.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.490
So here, I'm showing you
how you can use the Metrics

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:49.940
Explorer in
Stackdriver Monitoring

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.590
to further inspect any of
your company's objects.

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.370
So I'm looking at a pod, and
I'm going deeper into try

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.490
to understand this metric
of bytes transmitted.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:00.950
I can change the type of
graph that I have from aligned

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:03.750
to a stacked area, or to a bar.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.740
And then I can filter
down by metadata

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:10.460
that may be user-generated
or from the Kubernetes

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.030
environment.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:14.630
So I'm filtering down
here to my service,

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:19.530
and then I can further group
down into whatever I want.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.250
So there's a very simple and
intuitive, interactive way

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.754
of inspecting your
Kubernetes options.

00:05:24.754 --> 00:05:26.420
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
that's a great demo,

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:28.040
and a really elegant interface.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:32.240
I can tell a lot of work went
into the UX in this interface.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:35.100
What can you tell us at
this point about the pricing

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.422
for Stackdriver?

00:05:36.422 --> 00:05:38.630
JD VELASQUEZ: Oh, so
Stackdriver Companies Monitoring

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:41.600
follows the same pricing that we
just announced for Stackdriver,

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.630
and we have several principles
in defining this pricing.

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:46.640
First one is to make it
as simple as possible,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.290
so it's very easy
for our customers

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:52.910
to estimate what they use, and
forecast their spend and usage.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.110
Second, it's a
pay-as-you-go model.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.880
So depending on what metrics you
ingest into Stackdriver, what

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:00.280
logs you ingest
into Stackdriver,

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.870
or the traces you
have, then you have--

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:05.120
you get automatic
discounts based on volume.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:08.550
So all of this is following
that same simple model.

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.045
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: That's great.

00:06:10.045 --> 00:06:10.670
Well thank you.

00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:12.080
I hope you enjoy KubeCon.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:12.890
Thank you, Dustin.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.080
Thank you, JD.

